WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.258 --> 00:00:02.841
(upbeat music)

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.410
- Some people are firm on the notion

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:09.550
that social media is bad for society.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.930
Some say that it disconnects
us more than it connects us.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:16.370
They point to the increases
in depression and anxiety

00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:18.850
as reasons why it's not
good for our mental health.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:22.080
But I wanna give you one
example where social media

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.840
has had a fantastic impact on our society.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.010
In the midst of my social media fame,

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:30.920
I started a foundation
called Limitless Tomorrow

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.250
and my aim was to help other individuals

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.530
and even companies who
aspire to do great things

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.040
in this world to make
a change that affects

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.430
all of us as humans.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.120
The startup funding for this foundation

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:46.230
started through a unique collaboration

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:50.270
between Coffee Meets Bagel,
a dating app, and myself.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.780
I ended up auctioning
a date off for charity

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.110
and raised almost $100,000
for my new foundation.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:59.460
Now, a lot of people
have asked me questions,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.500
where did that money go?

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:01.660
Where does it go now?

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:04.550
This video highlights a prime example

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:07.330
of a person set out to change the world

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.430
in a positive way for us all.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:12.680
Meet Dr. Angelo Leto Barone.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:15.240
- Approximately 10 million
people overall in the world

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.100
suffer from various ear
and nose deformities.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.090
At ReconstratA we have a dream.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:22.880
We imagine a world where we
can easily treat the severe

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.620
facial deformities in an easy, reliable

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.300
and cost-effective way.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.270
So basically thanks to Limitless Tomorrow,

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.810
we were able to create a cookie cutter

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:33.230
for ear and nose reconstruction.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:35.460
- Dr. Leto Barone, can you
tell us what your current

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.880
position is and how you
figured out that you're

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.430
gonna go into your field of choice

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:42.550
because we have a lot of
medical students watching.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.210
They're trying to figure out what they

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:46.000
wanna do with their lives.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.900
They're curious to know about
all aspects of medicine,

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.680
so how did you get into the
the field that you're in now?

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.780
- Absolutely, so I was
actually six years old

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:57.780
and I saw this man go around
my town back home in Italy.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.550
He was going around, he had
a complete burn of his face.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:06.170
It was completely
disfigured and I was looking

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.310
at this person and I saw that he had lost

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.230
like a sense of self.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:13.420
So I just pulled my mom.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.610
I was like, who's the
doctor who does that,

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:16.750
who fixes that?

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:18.510
She was like, you know,
being a physician herself--

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:19.920
- Pretty impressive for a six-year-old.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:20.753
- Right.

00:02:20.753 --> 00:02:22.450
And she was like a plastic surgeon.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:23.870
I was like that's what I wanna be.

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:26.700
- But you have a unique journey
because you didn't just go

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.130
and do the specialty here
in the United States.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.950
You were born in Italy, right?

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:31.783
- Correct.

00:02:31.783 --> 00:02:33.040
- Palermo's your hometown.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:34.350
- Yep, where I went to medical school.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:35.183
- Nice.

00:02:35.183 --> 00:02:36.460
And how did you do that?

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:37.910
Did you go through
medical school right away?

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:39.280
What's the training in Italy like?

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.610
- So the training is you do
five years of high school

00:02:42.610 --> 00:02:44.320
and then it was very rigorous.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.830
And then you go straight
into medical school

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:46.780
which is six year.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.290
- Which is different
than what we have here

00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:50.010
in the United States
because we do high school,

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:51.580
university then medical school.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:52.413
- Correct.

00:02:52.413 --> 00:02:54.720
- And then in medical school, were you set

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.570
in saying I'm gonna
specialize in plastic surgery?

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:57.403
- I was set.

00:02:57.403 --> 00:02:59.370
Everyone kept saying you're
gonna change your mind

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:00.270
a thousand times.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:01.150
Didn't happen.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.120
I had my mission and I stuck with it.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:08.760
And I went to the University
of Palermo Medical School

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.280
then I was recruited to the residency

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:11.653
with the program there.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.830
And then I decided that--

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:16.710
- Now, was that a competitive process

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.520
because here in the
United States, if you want

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.910
a plastic surgery
residency, very difficult,

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:22.720
you have to be at the top of your class.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.690
As you know, if you're a foreign graduate,

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.280
it's very difficult to get a position

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:27.800
in plastic surgery residency.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.110
So how is that in Italy?

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:29.943
Does that compare?

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:32.090
- It's also very challenging.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:37.050
There is less spots and
also less people applying,

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.130
but at the same time, you can only apply

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:41.760
to one program at a time.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:42.593
- Wow.

00:03:42.593 --> 00:03:44.950
- So I applied to the only
program that I was interested in

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:46.950
and you either match or don't match,

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:48.770
so it's little bit more...

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:49.603
- Scarier.

00:03:49.603 --> 00:03:50.870
- Yep, a little scarier.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:54.083
I did a lot of research through residency.

00:03:55.185 --> 00:03:58.320
A few years I was at
Harvard, the Mass General,

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.021
and then I went back
home, finished residency

00:04:01.021 --> 00:04:03.410
and decided to come back and join

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.810
the Johns Hopkins University program.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.430
- Interesting, so what kind of research

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:08.930
did you do there while you were--

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.010
- It was mostly teaching engineering

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:14.660
and vascularized composite
allotransplantation which is--

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:16.380
- That's not complicated
at all, by the way,

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:17.730
totally easy to say.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:21.920
- It's a fancy word for face
and hand transplant research.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.970
So you know that these operations,

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:25.950
besides being extremely challenging,

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:30.320
they require a lot of immunosuppressants

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:33.070
to make sure that the body
doesn't reject the face

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:35.950
from the cadaver who donated it.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:36.990
- Very impressive stuff.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.100
You're working at Johns Hopkins.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:42.830
You're a happy but very tired
plastic surgery resident

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:46.580
and you wake up one morning
or you're in the OR one day

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.640
and you say I have an idea.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.340
Take me through that.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.560
- It was both in Italy
and the U.S. it started,

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:55.680
mostly in Italy.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.650
We were doing a case and we
were basically having problems

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.650
with the cartilage and lacking cartilage

00:05:03.721 --> 00:05:05.940
and I was thinking as
we were trying to treat

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.973
this microtia case, so this
kid was born without an ear,

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.890
and we were trying to carve
this ear out of rib cartilage,

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:16.183
which is a cartilage that
comes from the rib cage,

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:20.850
I realize that it was a process that was,

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.800
that entailed a great
amount of detail and skill

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.150
that probably most plastic
surgeons did not have,

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:30.750
despite going receive your training,

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:34.740
but it also required a very long surgery.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.220
And this is course
suboptimal for a patient

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:40.440
who's 10 years old and who has to undergo

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.750
a 12 hour operation.

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:46.073
So I was like, this is 2017
or 2015, whatever year it was,

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:51.670
2012, and we cannot do this,
we cannot have this operation

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:52.660
last this long.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:55.830
So I thought what if
somebody made a cookie cutter

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:56.960
that could allow us to
do this very quickly.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:57.850
- Do you like cookies,

00:05:57.850 --> 00:05:59.308
is that why you went to the cookie cutter?

00:05:59.308 --> 00:06:00.940
- I love cookies, I
like the Cookie Monster.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:03.080
- See, we never really
give cookies any benefit

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.430
because we always blame
diabetes on cookies,

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:07.940
all these issues, but look,
cookies can save the world

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.300
and clearly they were a
motivating factor here.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.560
- Absolutely, just like
playing with Play-Doh.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:13.393
- Exactly.

00:06:13.393 --> 00:06:14.840
- There was really a humanitarian mission

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.680
and purpose behind it.

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:20.090
In the U.S., it's easy to
perform such an operation

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.830
with these incredibly talented surgeons

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:24.980
and with the resources that we have,

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:26.610
but there are resources for countries

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:29.600
that cannot have either
access to plastic surgeons

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.923
with that training or have the

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:38.369
economical potential
to undergo the surgery.

00:06:38.369 --> 00:06:39.202
- Sure.

00:06:39.202 --> 00:06:40.670
- So we thought if we
make this cookie cutter,

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:42.560
we could actually go to these countries

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:47.460
and not only as a company
donate this device

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:50.230
to those hospitals, but
also bring our own set

00:06:50.230 --> 00:06:54.590
of physicians and train them
to perform the operation

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:59.010
without, basically giving them a fish,

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:00.700
but basically teaching them how to fish.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:04.840
One of my students who was
actually writing papers

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:07.740
with me and who was very
interested in plastic surgery,

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:11.260
who holds a biomedical
engineer degree from MIT,

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:12.093
told me one day, like, hey--

00:07:12.093 --> 00:07:13.400
- That's not impressive.

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:15.660
- Not impressive at all.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:18.090
Too bad he couldn't be here today.

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:21.683
So basically he was like,
do you have any ideas

00:07:21.683 --> 00:07:24.150
that we can actually bring to fruition

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:27.240
because he had like a
month off and I thought,

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:30.733
you know what, in Italy I
thought of this cookie cutter.

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:34.500
We need to really bring it to life.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.260
And we designed it together and we started

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.420
our Kickstarter campaign
which is actually how we

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:41.440
got in touch with you, looking for funds.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:43.490
- Today's a great day
because today's the first day

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:44.970
that I actually see your devise.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:46.160
- Yes, absolutely.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.360
- This magical device that can
change so many people's lives

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.910
and we have the device right here with us.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:53.680
This very beautiful project.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:55.930
Not only is it functional,
but it looks great,

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:57.520
so that whole aspect is there

00:07:57.520 --> 00:07:59.250
from a plastic surgery standpoint.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:00.310
So tell us a little bit about it.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:04.660
- Absolutely, so this is
AuryzoN and was mostly funded

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:07.000
through our Kickstarter
campaign as well as

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:08.420
the Limitless Tomorrow's funds

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:11.670
that you graciously awarded us.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:14.520
So it's basically modular components.

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:19.363
You have a top cassette that
is basically customizable

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:22.540
and then this would be anchored.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:23.870
There you go, super easy.

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:26.973
And then you would
remove the base cassette.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.650
You would select the cassette that has--

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:32.280
- There's sizes here if you don't see.

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:33.800
- Right, so this is a small ear,

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:35.820
which is a 48 millimeters, for example.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:37.480
- I have small ears.

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:39.680
- And give this gives basically an idea

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:43.620
of where the cartilage should
sit in order to be stamped

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.560
appropriately with the blade.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:47.640
You would just press.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.960
And once you, boom, once you raise it,

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.500
you have something that you
can suture together with wires.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:55.320
- Now how much time does
a process like this save

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:58.770
from the actual sculpting,
because there are surgeons

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:00.710
in the world that can do
it relatively quickly,

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:03.310
but you could probably
count them on one hand.

00:09:03.310 --> 00:09:06.300
- Right, I was lucky enough
to actually go to Dr. Nagata's

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:09.200
clinic in Japan, he's the
guru of ear reconstruction

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:12.103
and one of the surgeons
who advanced the field.

00:09:12.955 --> 00:09:16.010
So currently the gold
standard of reconstruction

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:18.560
is his technique, which
is what this allows to do

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.220
super quick, so Nagata
can do this operation

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:23.300
in about four, five hours.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.223
Most surgeons take up to 12 hours.

00:09:27.321 --> 00:09:30.180
And as you have mentioned before,

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:32.940
one minute in the OR cost $82.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:35.930
So if you consider 12
hours of an operation

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:40.370
of this kind, the cost
would be about $42, $45,000,

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:41.420
you guys do the math.

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:45.410
So this would allow it to carve everything

00:09:45.410 --> 00:09:48.030
at a very precise level
in a matter of seconds.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:49.020
- Excellent.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:51.100
- And then the assembling and then the--

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:52.040
- Actual attachment.

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:54.690
- The attachment to subcutaneous pocket

00:09:55.992 --> 00:09:57.330
would take probably like an hour or two.

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.160
- And now the other techniques that exist,

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:01.030
what's the different between those and--

00:10:01.030 --> 00:10:05.420
- So there is another
technique that uses a polymer

00:10:07.086 --> 00:10:11.156
and a synthetic ear
framework and that is--

00:10:11.156 --> 00:10:11.989
- So it's not human.

00:10:11.989 --> 00:10:12.822
- It's not human.

00:10:12.822 --> 00:10:14.550
- It's made out of different compounds

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:15.383
that are not found within the body.

00:10:15.383 --> 00:10:18.460
- Correct, it's basically
a biocompatible plastic.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:19.293
- Okay.

00:10:19.293 --> 00:10:23.110
- And you basically put
underneath the skin flaps

00:10:23.110 --> 00:10:24.380
and you create an ear.

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:27.510
Unfortunately, there results
are not always optimal

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:31.700
and not in everyone's hands are optimal.

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:34.210
And at the same time,
it's still a foreign body,

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:36.400
so it can give a lot of inflammation,

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:39.190
it can give infections, it can stick out

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:41.510
and we call that extrusion.

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:42.610
And when it sticks out,

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:44.640
usually the whole area gets infected.

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:48.200
You can go back to square one
or even to a worse scenario.

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:49.470
- Has there ever been a thought

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:51.450
about using donor application cartilage,

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:53.610
or maybe even animal cartilage?

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:58.160
You know pig cartilage is used
all over in medical stuff.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:10:58.993
- Absolutely.

00:10:58.993 --> 00:11:02.910
So, pig cartilage or cadaver cartilage

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:08.090
have a hurdle which is the
fact that pig cartilage

00:11:08.090 --> 00:11:10.430
is a xenotransplant, so it's a transplant

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:13.500
between two different
species and for that reason

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:16.040
it will be rejected.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:17.360
So it cannot be used.

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:19.910
So it would resorb and actually create,

00:11:19.910 --> 00:11:22.420
probably create an infection as well.

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:24.810
At the same time, cadaver cartilage,

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.550
which we use actually during
this kind of reconstruction,

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:31.610
has been irrigated and the
cells are not going to--

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:33.380
- They're not quality.

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:36.900
- Well they're not going to
give an immunological response,

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:39.252
so they're not gonna be
attacked by your own body.

00:11:39.252 --> 00:11:44.252
So it is used, sometimes it resorbs a lot

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:48.690
and often we did not get
enough of that in big pieces

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:51.900
to really make the ear,
so we really need to get

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:53.050
the patient's own ribs.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:53.883
